Backward compatibility: a single bare name (git-branch-status.sh foo)
behaved identically before and after -- the old code already expanded
the last argument. The real change is for the other arguments, which
used to be forwarded raw (matching nothing) and are now expanded too.

Considered requiring an explicit -- separator instead of auto-detecting
flags. Rejected it because a dash cannot begin a branch name, so the
flag-vs-branch split is unambiguous without one; -- is kept only as an
escape hatch for the rare non-refs/ positional pattern.

Edge case: an argument that begins with a dash *after* -- is expanded to
refs/heads/-foo. That is a grammar-legal ref but not a branch git will
create, so it is harmless nonsense rather than a supported input.

Not addressed here: both for-each-ref invocations receive "$@", so a
user-supplied --sort stacks with the hardcoded --sort=committerdate
rather than replacing it. Pre-existing; left alone.
